    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\( QS \\) in the given right triangle \\( SQR \\) where \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\), we need to recognize that \\( QS \\) is the adjacent side to angle \\( R \\) in the triangle. The hypotenuse of the right triangle \\( SQR \\) is the distance \\( QR \\), which we know is 9 units.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the length of the hypotenuse. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{QS}{QR}\n\\]\nGiven that \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\) and \\( QR = 9 \\), we can set up the following equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{QS}{9} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\nTo find \\( QS \\), we solve the equation by multiplying both sides by 9:\n\\[\nQS = 4\n\\]\nThus, the length of \\( QS \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
